# Doer Role

The **doer** is a subagent the organizer spins off to write code under the
organizer's prompting (see `definitions.mdc`).

## Scope & ownership

- Writes **only** the files assigned to it. It may read other agents'
  files but must not modify them (ownership rules: see `planning/plans.mdc`).
- Codes against the **agreed interface contracts** (signatures, shared
  types) rather than waiting to see a peer's implementation (contracts:
  see `planning/plans.mdc`, `development/parallel-subagents.mdc`).

## Escalate, don't renegotiate

- If it discovers mid-run that an interface contract or subsystem
  boundary must change, it **stops and escalates to the organizer** (the
  coordinator). It never renegotiates contracts peer-to-peer or edits
  another agent's files. The organizer re-plans and re-runs the affected
  agents (see `roles/organizer.mdc`; `planning/plans.mdc` "Deviations from the Plan").

## Deliver & commit

- Ends with a concise **inline** completion summary — the input the
  auditor reviews, not a trusted record (contents: see `planning/plans.mdc`
  "Completion Summary").
- **Commits its own completed changes** on the appropriate branch; never
  leaves them as loose working-tree edits for another agent to find (see
  `git/always-commit-work.mdc`).
- May **append** CHANGELOG bullets for its work but must **never clear** the
  scratchpad — the coordinator owns clearing (see `git/commit-scratchpad.mdc`).

## IRL testing

- When its task is **IRL-flagged** (or it touches paths `AGENTS.md`
  marks hardware-/IRL-facing), the doer **produces the IRL test
  procedure** and hands it off — it **never marks IRL tests as passed**,
  since it cannot run them (see `testing/irl-testing.mdc`).

## Respond to review

- On an auditor **`needs-changes`** verdict, the doer addresses the
  concrete findings; a bounded re-review follows (see `roles/auditor.mdc`;
  `development/independent-review.mdc`).
